WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
OAuth.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:07.700
It's behind every Sign in with Google button, and behind every tool that touches a user's accounts.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.167
OAuth is delegated authorization.

4
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:16.000
Your app obtains scoped tokens to act on a user's behalf at another service.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.500
Three parties are involved: the user, your app, and the provider's authorization server.

6
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.100
Let's walk the handshake.

7
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.433
One: your app redirects the browser to the provider, naming its client id and the scopes it wants.

8
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:34.736
Two: the user logs in and consents on the provider's own page.

9
00:00:34.736 --> 00:00:36.867
The password is typed only there.

10
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:42.233
Three: the provider redirects back with a short lived, one time authorization code.

11
00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:47.440
Four: your server exchanges that code, plus its client secret, for tokens.

12
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.567
Server to server.

13
00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.367
Notice what never happens.

14
00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:54.600
The password is typed only on the provider's page.

15
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.100
Your app ends up with a scoped token, never the password.

16
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.300
You come away with two kinds of token.

17
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.133
The access token is scoped and expiring.

18
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:08.800
And the refresh token mints new access tokens without asking the user again.

19
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.367
Here's the flow in code.

20
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:16.767
Step one sends the browser to the provider, asking for a code and specific scopes.

21
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:19.967
The provider redirects back with that one time code.

22
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:25.700
Then our server posts the code and client secret to the token endpoint, and gets tokens back.

23
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.800
Here's why this matters for agents.

24
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.767
Your research agent has a tool that adds asteroid close approach dates to a user's calendar.

25
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:40.200
The user consents once to a calendar events scope, and your server stores the refresh token.

26
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.481
The agent acts with a token that can do only that.

27
00:01:43.481 --> 00:01:46.700
Even if the model tried, it couldn't read their email.

28
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:48.780
Let's recap.

29
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.200
OAuth is delegated authorization.

30
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.833
The user logs in only at the provider.

31
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:58.267
An authorization code is exchanged for tokens, server to server.

32
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:01.764
And scopes keep an agent's power limited.

33
00:02:01.764 --> 00:02:02.933
Next up: J W T.
